LR  - 20190716
IS  - 0002-922X (Print)
IS  - 0002-922X (Linking)
VI  - 133
IP  - 6
DP  - 1979 Jun
TI  - Epidemic occurrence of neonatal necrotizing enterocolitis.
PG  - 594-7
AB  - In case-control studies of three epidemics of neonatal necrotizing enterocolitis 
      (NEC) in three different high-risk nurseries in three states, no particular risk 
      factor was associated with affected infants or their mothers. Epidemic cases had 
      higher birth weights and Apgar scores and fewer perinatal difficulties than those 
      previously reported for sporadic cases. Seven infants fed primarily breast milk 
      were not protected against disease. Early antibiotic therapy was associated with 
      a significantly decreased risk of disease in one outbreak. In two hospitals, 
      affected infants who received antibiotic therapy during the first three days of 
      life had a significantly later disease onset. The occurrence of the disease in 
      epidemics and the decreased risk or modification of disease with antibiotic 
      therapy support an infectious etiology for NEC.
