TI  - Neonatal Early-Onset Sepsis: Epidemiology, Microbiology, and Controversies in 
      Practice.
PG  - 15-31
LID - S0095-5108(24)00102-7 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.clp.2024.10.002 [doi]
AB  - Early-onset sepsis (EOS) remains a substantial contributor to neonatal morbidity 
      and mortality. Continued epidemiologic surveillance of incidence, risk factors, 
      and microbiology is paramount to developing new prevention strategies and 
      optimizing antibiotic administration. Understanding the risks and benefits of 
      maternal antibiotic exposure and neonatal risk assessment can inform clinical 
      management. Maternal vaccination during pregnancy is a promising avenue for EOS 
      prevention, particularly against group B Streptococcus. When EOS is suspected, 
      ampicillin and gentamicin are the appropriate routine empiric regimen in most 
      cases. Finally, a deeper understanding of the existing disparities in EOS can 
      shed light on how to provide more equitable care.
