PMID- 30335297
STAT- Publisher
CTDT- 20260914
PB  - StatPearls Publishing
DP  - 2026 Jan
TI  - Neonatal Meningitis.
BTI - StatPearls
AB  - Neonatal meningitis remains a serious infection of the central nervous system and 
      is associated with significant morbidity and mortality despite improvements in 
      obstetric care, neonatal intensive care, and preventive strategies. Survivors are 
      at risk for long-term complications, including hearing impairment, seizures, 
      cognitive deficits, and neurodevelopmental delay. Early diagnosis remains 
      challenging because clinical manifestations are often nonspecific and may overlap 
      with other neonatal conditions. Furthermore, the epidemiology of neonatal 
      meningitis varies according to gestational age, timing of disease onset, 
      geographic region, and local antimicrobial resistance patterns. Diagnostic 
      evaluation can also be complicated by prior antibiotic exposure, which may reduce 
      culture yield and obscure cerebrospinal fluid findings. Results from recent 
      studies suggest that the burden of disease may be underestimated, particularly in 
      resource-limited settings where access to advanced microbiologic testing is 
      limited. Neonatal susceptibility to meningitis is largely related to the immature 
      immune system, with preterm infants being particularly vulnerable. Pathogens 
      typically gain access to the central nervous system through hematogenous spread 
      following maternal, nosocomial, or community acquisition. In high-income 
      countries, group B Streptococcus and Escherichia coli organisms remain the most 
      common causes of bacterial neonatal meningitis, although the incidence of 
      Listeria monocytogenes infection has declined in many regions. Emerging 
      multidrug-resistant gram-negative organisms have become an increasing concern, 
      particularly among hospitalized infants and those with very low birth weight. 
      Viral pathogens, including herpesviruses, enteroviruses, and parechoviruses, also 
      contribute to neonatal meningitis and may be clinically indistinguishable from 
      bacterial meningitis. Although less common, fungal pathogens should also be 
      considered in the differential diagnosis. Because delayed treatment is associated 
      with worse outcomes, current recommendations support a low threshold for lumbar 
      puncture in neonates with suspected sepsis or clinical features concerning for 
      central nervous system infection.
