VI  - 88
IP  - 11
DP  - 1963 Mar 16
TI  - Listeria monocytogenes infections in Metropolitan Toronto. A clinicopathological 
      study.
PG  - 549-61
AB  - The clinical and laboratory findings in 21 patients with listeriosis are 
      described and the subject is reviewed. Eleven of the infections were septicemias 
      of newborns, eight were meningitis in infants or adults, and two other children 
      had unusual manifestations.Neonatal septicemia was rapidly fatal; one of 11 
      infants survived. The disease often seemed traceable to mild maternal infection 
      during the third trimester usually leading to premature delivery of critically 
      ill babies. Only awareness of the possible presence of listeriosis and early 
      antibiotic therapy seem capable of reducing this high mortality.Tissues from 
      autopsies showed characteristic microscopic necrotic foci with mononuclear 
      infiltration progressing to microabscesses containing small Gram-positive rods. 
      Lesions were found in the one placenta examined.Five infants with meningitis 
      recovered, and one of three affected adults. Specific diagnosis depends on 
      demonstrating Listeria monocytogenes; differentiation from other forms of acute 
      meningitis cannot be made clinically.One older child had septicemia and another 
      had listerial pharyngitis. Both recovered.
